title: 14. Effects and capabilities (`uses fs`, `provide`/`context`)
nav: Learn
order: 1400
template: docs
---
<p>A function signature in Bayan tells you what the function returns. It also tells you what the function <em>does to the outside world</em>: whether it prints, reads files, uses the network, asks for the time, or allocates. These are called <em>effects</em>. The compiler checks them, so a signature cannot hide a file read.</p>

<h2 id="pure">Pure by default</h2>
<p>A function with no <code>uses</code> clause does nothing but compute. It cannot print, read a file or allocate. That makes it easy to test and safe to call from anywhere.</p>
<p>The effects are <code>alloc</code>, <code>suspend</code>, <code>net</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>clock</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>process</code> and <code>console</code>. You list the ones a function needs after the parameters:</p>
<example name="book-14-effects"/>
<p>Look at each function in turn.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>square</code> is pure.</li>
<li><code>squares</code> builds a list, so it says <code>uses alloc</code>.</li>
<li><code>show</code> prints and walks a list. It says <code>uses store</code>, where <code>store</code> is an alias declared on the first line: <code>effect store = console, alloc</code>. An alias is only a short name for a set of effects.</li>
<li><code>traced</code> calls <code>log(...)</code>. Logging is allowed anywhere, so <code>traced</code> stays pure. The text goes to standard error, which is why it shows as <code>info: tracing 1</code> and not in the program's normal output.</li>
<li><code>main</code> uses <code>console</code> and <code>alloc</code> because it calls <code>print</code> and builds a list.</li>
</ul>
<p>Some standard library calls and their effects: <code>print</code> uses <code>console</code>, <code>Io.readText</code> uses <code>fs</code>, and <code>Io.monotonicNanos()</code> uses <code>clock</code>.</p>

<h2 id="checked">Public functions must declare their effects</h2>
<p>Inside one file, the compiler works out effects for private helpers. A <code>public</code> function is part of a module's contract, so it has to state its effects. Leave one out and you get this:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public void greet(string name) {
    print($"hello {name}")
}

void main() uses console {
    greet("Sam")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6002:</span> `greet` uses `console` but does not declare it
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> missing-uses.byn:2:5

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     <span class="hl">print($"hello {name}")</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>     <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ uses `console`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `console` to the `uses` clause, or remove the call
<span class="gut">    │ 1 │</span> public void greet(string name)<span class="add"> uses console</span> {
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>                               <span class="add">+++++++++++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN6002</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened to <code>missing-uses.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The error points at the call that needs the effect. The fix-it shows the exact clause to add. Calling a function that uses an effect makes the caller use it too, all the way up to <code>main</code>.</p>
<p>A misspelled effect has its own error. It also lists every valid name:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public void greet(string name) uses printing {
    print($"hello {name}")
}

void main() uses console {
    greet("Sam")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6003:</span> `printing` is not an effect
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> unknown-effect.byn:1:37

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> public void greet(string name) uses <span class="hl">printing</span> {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                     <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^ not an effect or an `effect` alias of this module</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> choose one of `alloc`, `suspend`, `net`, `fs`, `clock`, `random`, `env`, `process`,
        `console`, or a declared effect alias

<span class="err">error BYN6002:</span> `greet` uses `console` but does not declare it
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> unknown-effect.byn:2:5

<span class="gut">  2 │</span>     <span class="hl">print($"hello {name}")</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>     <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ uses `console`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `console` to the `uses` clause, or remove the call
<span class="gut">    │ 1 │</span> public void greet(string name) uses printing<span class="add">, console</span> {
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>                                             <span class="add">+++++++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 2 errors, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN6003</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened to <code>unknown-effect.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>

<h2 id="capabilities">Capabilities: the package decides</h2>
<p>Effects are what code <em>asks</em> for. A package also states what it is <em>allowed</em> to do, in <code>capabilities</code> in <code>package.byn</code>. A new project from <code>bayan new</code> starts with none:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Package {
    name: "local/demo",
    version: "0.1.0",
    edition: 2027,
    kind: .app,
    capabilities: [],
    deps: [],
    dist: Dist { targets: [.host], mode: .safe },
}</code></pre>
<p>Now add a function that reads a file:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public string load(string path) uses fs, alloc fails IoError {
    return try Io.readText(path)
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6004:</span> `load` uses `fs`, which package `local/demo` does not declare in `capabilities`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> main.byn:1:33

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> public string load(string path) <span class="hl">uses fs, alloc</span> fails IoError {
<span class="gut">    │</span>                                 <span class="hl">^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ uses `fs`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `.fs` to `capabilities`, or remove the use

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN6004</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the v0.1 compiler. The path is shortened to <code>capabilities.txt</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>Do as the help says: write <code>capabilities: [.fs]</code>. The list is a summary of what the package can touch, and it is checked at build time. Anyone who reads <code>package.byn</code> sees at a glance whether a library can reach the network or the disk.</p>

<h2 id="provide">Choosing where memory comes from: provide</h2>
<p><code>provide</code> installs a handler for a block of code. Chapter 13 used it for the allocator:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">using var arena = Arena()
provide allocator: arena {
    // every `uses alloc` call in here allocates from the arena
}</code></pre>
<p>In this compiler release only the allocator handlers work: <code>provide alloc</code> and <code>provide allocator: …</code>. Trying one for another effect, such as <code>console</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>clock</code> or <code>net</code>, is rejected with BYN2022, "handler is not supported in v0.1". An unknown name is BYN6007.</p>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. Handlers for the other effects, so a test could <code>provide clock: fixedClock { … }</code> and run code against a fake clock or a fake file system. Also <code>context</code> values, which carry a setting such as a logger down the call chain without passing it as a parameter. A <code>suspend</code> effect is accepted in <code>uses</code>, but nothing can suspend yet; see <a href="book-17-concurrency.html">chapter 17</a>.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">// planned
provide clock: Clock.fixed(1_000) {
    check(expired(token))
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>1. In <code>book-14-effects</code>, why does <code>traced</code> declare no <code>uses</code> even though it calls <code>log</code>?</summary>
<p>Logging is allowed in any function and is not an effect you declare. That is why it prints <code>info: tracing 1</code> on standard error and the program output shows only <code>traced 2</code>. It is not because <code>log</code> does nothing. Nor is it because <code>traced</code> is private: it is <code>public</code>, which would require a declaration for <code>console</code>.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>2. A public function calls <code>print</code> and has no <code>uses</code>. What does the compiler say?
<ol type="a"><li>Nothing, <code>print</code> is built in.</li><li>BYN6002, with a fix-it that adds <code>uses console</code>.</li><li>BYN6003, because <code>print</code> is not an effect.</li></ol></summary>
<p>b. The first is wrong: <code>print</code> uses <code>console</code>. The third mixes up errors: BYN6003 is for a name in the <code>uses</code> clause that is not an effect, like <code>uses printing</code>.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>3. What does <code>effect store = console, alloc</code> do?</summary>
<p>It gives a name to a set of effects, so <code>uses store</code> means <code>uses console, alloc</code>. It does not create a new capability or install a handler.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>4. The code says <code>uses fs</code> and the project builds with BYN6004. What do you change?</summary>
<p>Add <code>.fs</code> to <code>capabilities</code> in <code>package.byn</code>, or remove the file access. Adding <code>uses fs</code> to more functions does not help: the function already declares it. The package is the one that has not agreed to it.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>5. Can you write <code>provide console: myConsole { … }</code> today?</summary>
<p>No. Only <code>alloc</code> and <code>allocator:</code> handlers work now. Others give BYN2022. The feature is planned.</p></details>
